            <ArticleTitle>Murine model of chronic human asthma.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>141-4</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Human asthma is associated with acute and chronic inflammation of the airway mucosa, accompanied by airway wall remodelling. Experimental models of acute allergic bronchopulmonary inflammation in mice are useful for investigation of immunological mechanisms and of cellular recruitment, but have significant limitations because they fail to reproduce a number of characteristic lesions of human asthma, while usually being associated with marked alveolitis. We have developed an improved murine model of asthma that exhibits almost all of the morphological and functional changes that typify the human disease, without any confounding alveolitis. This model has considerable potential for the investigation of pathogenetic mechanisms and potential treatments of chronic human asthma.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
